---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ScientificCalculator from '@/components/tools/math/ScientificCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="scientific-calculator">
  <ScientificCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the scientific calculator</h2>
    <p>
      Type an expression into the box and press Enter, or use the keypad. The result updates as you type.
      The calculator works on whole expressions, not button sequences, so you can read back exactly what you
      calculated and edit it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Enter</strong> evaluates the expression and adds the line to the history tape.</li>
      <li><strong>Escape</strong> clears the expression.</li>
      <li>Typing anywhere on the page goes into the expression box.</li>
      <li>A click on a history line loads that expression again for editing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Functions the parser understands</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Names</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Trigonometry</td><td>sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2</td><td><code>sin(30)</code> → 0.5 in degree mode</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hyperbolic</td><td>sinh, cosh, tanh, asinh, acosh, atanh</td><td><code>cosh(1)</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Logs and powers</td><td>ln, log, log2, log10, exp</td><td><code>log(2, 1024)</code> → 10</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Roots</td><td>sqrt, cbrt, root(n, x)</td><td><code>root(5, 32)</code> → 2</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rounding</td><td>round, floor, ceil, trunc, abs, sign</td><td><code>round(3.14159, 2)</code> → 3.14</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Other</td><td>mod, min, max, hypot, fact</td><td><code>hypot(3, 4)</code> → 5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Constants</td><td>pi, e, tau, phi</td><td><code>2pi</code> → 6.28318530718</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Memory</td><td>ans, mem</td><td><code>ans * 2</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>log(x)</code> with one argument is the base-10 logarithm; with two arguments,
      <code>log(base, x)</code>, it is the logarithm in that base. Multiplication can be implicit:
      <code>2(3+4)</code>, <code>3pi</code> and <code>2sin(45)</code> all work. A trailing <code>!</code> is
      factorial and a trailing <code>%</code> divides by 100, so <code>50%</code> is 0.5 and
      <code>5!</code> is 120.
    </p>

    <h2>Operator precedence</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Brackets</li>
      <li>Function calls, factorial and percent</li>
      <li>Powers (<code>^</code>), evaluated right to left, so <code>2^3^2</code> is 2⁹ = 512</li>
      <li>Unary minus</li>
      <li>Multiplication and division, left to right</li>
      <li>Addition and subtraction, left to right</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <code>-2^2</code> is −4: by the usual mathematical convention, the power binds more tightly than the
      sign. Write <code>(-2)^2</code> to get 4.
    </p>

    <h2>Degrees and radians</h2>
    <p>
      The DEG/RAD key sets the angle mode for sin, cos, tan and their inverses. In degree mode
      <code>sin(30)</code> is 0.5; in radian mode it is −0.988. Each line in the history tape records the
      active mode, so a copied tape is unambiguous. Hyperbolic functions do not use the mode, because their
      argument is never an angle.
    </p>
    <p>
      Values that must be exactly zero, such as <code>sin(180)</code> in degree mode, are rounded to 0 and not
      shown as 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁶. Real asymptotes such as <code>tan(90)</code> give an error in place of a huge
      meaningless number.
    </p>

    <h2>Using the memory keys</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>MS</td><td>Store the current result in memory, replacing what was there</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MR</td><td>Insert <code>mem</code> into the expression, recalling the stored value</td></tr>
          <tr><td>M+</td><td>Add the current result to the stored value</td></tr>
          <tr><td>M−</td><td>Subtract the current result from the stored value</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MC</td><td>Clear the memory back to zero</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The stored value is always shown next to the result.
    </p>

    <h2>No eval, and no network</h2>
    <p>
      A tokeniser and a recursive-descent parser written for this page process the expression. There is no
      call to <code>eval</code> and no <code>Function</code> constructor. So code typed in the input box cannot
      run, and a syntax error points at the exact character that caused it. Arithmetic uses IEEE 754 double
      precision, about 15 significant digits. Results show 12 digits, so floating-point noise such as
      0.30000000000000004 does not appear in the answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keystrokes, results and history stay in this tab. When you close the tab, the tape is gone.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
